# Env names only — never values. Secrets travel vault -> render -> env,
# never through git.
#
# One block per plugin that exists in this repo. Later waves add their own;
# do not pre-declare names for plugins that are not here yet.

# ── notes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# notes-srv has NO users. NOTES_API_KEY is both the credential AND the
# top-level folder this profile may write under (key `claude` -> writes
# `claude` and `claude/...`, nothing else). Each profile needs its OWN key:
# a shared one means every agent writes into one folder as one identity.
# There is deliberately no NOTES_USER_ID — there is no user to identify.
# NOTES_URL=
# NOTES_API_KEY=

# ── fin3-bridge ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FIN3_USER_ID is absent on purpose in the default `fin3` profile: without it
# every forwarded call fails the server-side identity check, so the default
# can act for nobody.
# FIN3_URL=
# FIN3_TOOL_KEY=
# FIN3_USER_ID=

# ── vita3-bridge ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VITA3_URL=
# VITA3_TOOL_KEY=
# VITA3_USER_ID=

# ── recipes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# recipe-srv is a single-user LAN app with NO auth — no key, no user id.
# RECIPES_URL=

# ── home ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# home-srv-v2 requires BOTH: the key proves the caller is a trusted tool
# caller, the user id says whom it acts for. HOME_USER_ID comes from the
# profile env so the model can neither see nor set it; a profile without one
# gets a 401 and acts for nobody.
# HOME_URL=
# HOME_API_KEY=   # same name the app and the gate already use
# HOME_USER_ID=

# ── weather ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Open-Meteo is KEYLESS. There is no WEATHER_API_KEY; don't go looking.
# WEATHER_LAT=
# WEATHER_LON=

# ── radarr ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Third-party, single-tenant: one shared library, so no user id.
# RADARR_URL=
# RADARR_API_KEY=

# ── sonarr / lidarr ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SONARR_URL=
# SONARR_API_KEY=
# LIDARR_URL=
# LIDARR_API_KEY=

# ── plex ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PLEX_URL=
# PLEX_TOKEN=

# ── tmdb ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Key travels as a QUERY PARAM, so it is redacted from error messages.
# TMDB_API_KEY=

# ── nzb ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NZB_URL needs its TRAILING SLASH. The password is in the URL path, so the
# URL is redacted from every error message.
# NZB_URL=
# NZB_USER=
# NZB_PASSWORD=

# ── minimax ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MINIMAX_API_KEY=

# ── tickets / cal ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The KEY is the identity — looked up in the shared identity table, and
# username and apiKey are the same value by convention. Each profile gets its
# OWN key; no user id is sent at all. Both apps gained key auth on 2026-08-05
# (tickets-srv 96df76c, cal-srv cd38b84); before that they trusted a bare
# X-User-Id header and neither plugin could safely exist.
# TICKETS_URL=
# TICKETS_API_KEY=
# CAL_URL=
# CAL_API_KEY=
